 our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/c5/c5383ad51988564756ba754cbd6107f487fd0b32be0013f8a89bb78a6900f540.png)" 13194139533577 17592186045417 false] [17592186119672 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, 